{
  "id": "present-continuous-now",
  "level": "a1",
  "area": "Here and now",
  "title": "Present continuous: what is happening now",
  "tagline": "am/is/are + -ing — for the exact moment you're speaking.",
  "cefr": "A1",
  "exam": [
    "Listening Part 1 — a picture and five short conversations",
    "Speaking Part 1 — describing what's happening now"
  ],
  "why": "The present continuous is the tense for right now — what people are doing at this exact moment, in a photo or in a conversation. It's everywhere in Listening Part 1, where you match a picture to what people are doing. Get the -ing spelling right and this tense is easy.",
  "hook": {
    "text": "The class is in Cusco! Miss Vega takes a photo. \"Look — Mateo <b>is climbing</b> the steps, Sofia <b>is taking</b> photos, and Liam and Nadia <b>are eating</b> ice cream. What <b>are</b> you <b>doing</b>?\" \"I<b>'m watching</b> all of you!\"",
    "ask": "Find five -ing verbs in the story. Which words tell you the action is happening right now?"
  },
